CAT = "ausecLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
# CPPSEC2102 knowledge evidence (types of law, rules of evidence, continuity, scene preservation,
# court protocol, duty of care, licensing and limits of authority); Private Security Act 2004 (Vic)
# ss 128 and 176 for the two labelled Victorian items.
Q = [
("Which type of law is made by parliament?",
 "Statute law", ["Common law from courts", "Case law from judges", "Customary local law"],
 "Statute law is written law passed by a parliament as Acts. Common law, also called case law, is built up from judges' decisions."),
("A shopper sues a shopping centre after slipping on a wet floor. Which type of law applies?",
 "Civil law", ["Criminal law", "Statute law alone", "Military law"],
 "A dispute between private parties over a wrong such as negligence is civil law; the remedy is usually money damages, not a penalty from the state."),
("Theft and assault are prosecuted by the state under which type of law?",
 "Criminal law", ["Civil law", "Contract law", "Industrial relations law"],
 "Criminal law deals with offences against the community, prosecuted by the state, which can lead to fines or jail."),
("Law that comes from judges' decisions in earlier cases is called:",
 "Common law", ["Statute law", "Delegated legislation", "Licence conditions"],
 "Common law grows from precedent: courts follow earlier decisions on similar facts. Statute law is passed by parliament."),
("When should an officer write up notes of an incident?",
 "As soon as practicable", ["At the end of the week", "Only if police ask for them", "After the client approves them"],
 "Notes made at the time or straight after (contemporaneous notes) carry the most weight in court, because memory fades and details get mixed up."),
("A guard writes: \"The man looked like a drug dealer.\" What is wrong with this entry?",
 "It is opinion", ["It is far too short", "It gives no time of day", "It uses the past tense"],
 "Courts want what the officer saw and heard, not conclusions. Write the facts, such as what the man did and said, and let the court draw the inference."),
("A witness tells you what her friend saw. In court, your account of that is mostly:",
 "Hearsay", ["Direct evidence", "Expert opinion evidence", "Real evidence"],
 "Evidence of what someone else told you, offered to prove the thing they saw, is hearsay and is generally not admitted. The friend should give her own evidence."),
("What does continuity of evidence mean?",
 "A record of everyone who held an item", ["Keeping all your notes in a single book", "Giving evidence without any break", "Telling the same story twice"],
 "Continuity shows the court that an exhibit could not have been swapped or changed: every person who held it, when and where, is recorded."),
("You find a knife on the floor after a fight. Police are on their way. What should you do?",
 "Leave it and guard the area", ["Bag it and lock it in your locker", "Wipe it and hand it to the manager", "Kick it under a table"],
 "Preserve the scene: do not move or handle the item unless it is in danger of being lost or used. Keep people away and point it out to police."),
("Why cordon off an area where an assault happened?",
 "To protect evidence", ["To punish the people involved in it", "To stop the media from filming", "To hide the scene from the client"],
 "Footprints, blood, glass and fibres are easily trodden on or moved. A cordon keeps everyone out until police take over."),
("You must step into a crime scene to help an injured person. What is the right approach?",
 "Help them, then note what you moved", ["Wait for police before helping", "Clean up the scene, then help", "Help only once police approve it"],
 "Life comes first. Give aid, keep disturbance to a minimum, and record anything moved and why so police can account for it."),
("Giving evidence in court, you do not hear a question clearly. You should:",
 "Ask for it to be repeated", ["Guess what the lawyer meant", "Answer a similar question instead", "Stay silent until they move on"],
 "Answer only what you understand. Asking for the question again is normal; guessing can make you look unreliable."),
("Which is the best way to answer questions in the witness box?",
 "Briefly and truthfully", ["At length, to impress the lawyer", "Loudly, toward the public gallery", "Casually, facing the defendant"],
 "Keep answers short and factual and direct them to the magistrate or jury. Do not argue with counsel."),
("What is duty of care for a security officer?",
 "Taking reasonable care not to harm others", ["Guaranteeing that nobody is ever hurt", "Caring only for the client's own staff", "Obeying every customer's request"],
 "Duty of care is the legal duty to avoid acts or omissions you can reasonably foresee would harm people, including patrons, staff and people you remove."),
("A patron you removed staggers drunk toward a busy road. Duty of care means you should:",
 "Act to reduce the danger", ["Ignore him, he is off the premises", "Walk him across the road quickly", "Hold his wallet so it is not lost"],
 "Your duty does not end at the door if your actions created or added to a foreseeable risk. Get help, call a friend, a taxi or 000 as needed."),
("Who issues a security licence in Australia?",
 "State or territory regulator", ["The Commonwealth Government", "The registered training organisation", "Your security employer"],
 "Each state and territory licenses its own security workers, for example through its police force or fair trading body. The RTO issues the training record, not the licence."),
("Your licence covers unarmed guarding only. The manager asks you to work as a crowd controller. You:",
 "Decline that duty", ["Do it for one night only", "Do it if the manager signs a note", "Do it without wearing a uniform"],
 "You may only carry out the activities your licence class authorises. Working outside it is an offence for you and the person who engaged you."),
("A licensed guard moves from Victoria to Queensland. What applies?",
 "Queensland's own licence rules", ["The Victorian licence covers every state", "No licence is needed for a year", "The RTO certificate replaces a licence"],
 "Licences are issued state by state. Some states recognise interstate licences under mutual recognition or permits, so ask the new state's regulator first."),
("In Victoria, who may ask a guard on duty to produce the licence?",
 "Police or the person being served", ["Only a police officer", "Any passer-by on the street at all", "Only the guard's own employer or manager"],
 "Section 128 of the Private Security Act 2004 (Vic) lets a police officer or authorised person require it, and the holder must also produce it to a person they are providing services to."),
("In Victoria, a licensed guard is charged with an offence. When must the Chief Commissioner be told?",
 "Within 7 days of the charge", ["Only after a conviction", "At the next licence renewal", "Within 30 days of the hearing"],
 "Section 176 of the Private Security Act 2004 (Vic) requires written notice within 7 days of being charged, with the hearing date and place. Other states have their own rules."),
("Why must a guard know the limits of their own authority?",
 "Overstepping them can be unlawful", ["It lets them refuse any duty they dislike", "It sets their hourly rate of pay", "It decides their uniform"],
 "A guard has no police powers. Going past what the law and the site instructions allow can turn a lawful act into assault or false imprisonment."),
("A client asks you to search a worker's locker with no consent or policy. You should:",
 "Refuse and refer it upward", ["Search it quietly after hours", "Search it and tell nobody", "Search it if the client insists"],
 "Searching property without consent or lawful authority can be trespass or unlawful. Raise it through your supervisor and the client's written procedures."),
("What are a site's standard operating procedures for?",
 "How tasks are done on that site", ["Replacing the general law while on site", "Listing every staff member's salary", "Advertising the company"],
 "SOPs, also called assignment instructions, tell you what to do on this site within the law. They never override legislation."),
("A security officer refuses entry because of a patron's ethnic background. This is:",
 "Unlawful discrimination", ["A valid house rule", "Allowed if the manager agrees", "Allowed after midnight"],
 "Anti-discrimination law prohibits refusing service or entry because of race. Refusals must rest on lawful grounds such as intoxication or behaviour."),
("Personal information about patrons gathered on shift should be:",
 "Shared only on a need-to-know basis", ["Posted in the staff group chat for everyone", "Given to anyone who asks politely", "Kept on your own phone"],
 "Privacy law and site policy limit who may see personal information. Share it only with people who need it for their role or with lawful authority."),
("Which record could later be used as evidence in court?",
 "Incident report", ["Weekly shift roster", "Uniform order form", "Staff pay slip"],
 "Incident reports and notebooks record what happened and can be produced in court, so they must be accurate, factual and complete."),
("You made a mistake in your notebook. How should you correct it?",
 "One line through it, initialled", ["Tear the page out of the book", "Cover it with correction fluid", "Write over it in darker pen"],
 "A single line keeps the original readable and shows the change was honest. Removing pages or hiding text suggests tampering."),
("A court case calls you as a witness. What should you wear?",
 "Neat, professional clothing", ["Whatever feels most comfortable", "Casual weekend clothes", "Clothing with the client's brand"],
 "Personal presentation is part of court protocol in CPPSEC2102. Dress neatly; a tidy uniform or business clothes is usual."),
]
